  "image_name": "c0c0c324770701dc2dba994c5bb48bdb.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how particles can be transformed by changes in state, solubility, and surface behavior to produce new shapes or composite structures. Across the top, “particle state” examples show heating, stretching, crosslinking, and release steps that convert simple particles into elongated, bent, clustered, or faceted forms. Some pathways involve melting or softening and then resolidifying, while others use plasticization to reshape clusters or fused particles, including hybrid particles made from different materials.\n\nA second group focuses on “particle solubility,” illustrating how dissolution or swelling can hollow particles, create dimples, shrink networks, add swollen patches, inflate shells, or cause buckling. These examples emphasize solvent-driven inward or outward deformation rather than bulk melting.\n\nA third section highlights “particle surfaces,” where interfacial effects control restructuring. Visible mechanisms include dewetting, growth of a liquid phase between particles, surfactant organization, photopatterning, and cyclic assembly or release. Together, the figure conveys that particle morphology is highly tunable through thermal, chemical, and interfacial processes, enabling transitions from simple spheres to anisotropic, hollow, fused, patchy, and reconfigurable colloidal architectures.",
